    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **James Wood** stands as our premier offensive anchor with an elite efficiency score of 78.42. His matchup against Kelly presents a massive opportunity, bolstered by a highly favorable 4.74 xERA and a 0.401 xwOBA that suggests he is primed for a multi-hit performance. We are also leaning heavily on **Brice Turang** (73.40) and **Seiya Suzuki** (67.57); **Brice Turang** benefits from a lucrative combination of Feltner\u2019s 4.52 xERA and a significant park factor boost, while **Seiya Suzuki** thrives despite the tough matchup against Ray, as his strong platoon profile and spot in the cleanup hole provide a high floor for run production.\n\nThe benching decision for **Zach McKinstry** was the closest call of the morning. While he holds some utility, his score of 38.03 landed below our 40-point \"Zebras\" floor, primarily due to the poor matchup against Woo and a negative impact from his spot in the bottom third of the order. Meanwhile, we remain cautious regarding **Corbin Carroll** (61.72) and **Austin Riley** (58.74); both face elite defensive hurdles\u2014specifically catchers with sub-1.90s pop times\u2014that complicate their stolen base and extra-base hit potential. While **Tyler Soderstrom** offers a strong 65.86 play given his lineup protection and platoon advantage, we must monitor the weather and any late-breaking lineup changes for **Cole Young**, whose aggressive base-running upside is vital to our stolen base strategy, even if his actual score of 51.35 makes him a marginal start. With **Mickey Moniak**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Jose Caballero**, **Trevor Story**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Max Muncy**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Alex Freeland**, **Kyle Karros**, and **Carson Williams** unavailable or restricted, our active corps must carry the load to maintain our competitive edge in this Roto cycle.",
